# Balancia configuration.
#
# For Docker Compose, `./scripts/bootstrap.sh` writes the two required secrets
# into .env for you, and — run from a terminal — asks about the optional
# features and writes those answers too. Everything else here is optional —
# copy what you need from this file into that .env to override a default.
#
# For local development without Docker, copy to .env.local and set at least
# DATABASE_URL and AUTH_SECRET.
#
# Full reference: docs/environment.md

# ── Required for a domain-based installation ────────────────────────────────

# The public URL people type into the browser. Must be https:// unless it is
# localhost — WebAuthn (passkeys) refuses to work otherwise.
APP_URL=https://balancia.example.com

# WebAuthn relying-party ID. Defaults to the APP_URL host, which is correct for
# most installs. Set it explicitly only to share passkeys across subdomains
# (e.g. "example.com" for app.example.com).
# WEBAUTHN_RP_ID=example.com

# Name shown in the browser's passkey prompt.
# WEBAUTHN_RP_NAME=Balancia

# Extra origins allowed to call the auth API, comma separated. Rarely needed.
# TRUSTED_ORIGINS=https://alt.example.com

# ── Secrets ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Required by Compose, and written for you by ./scripts/bootstrap.sh. Set them
# by hand only if you manage secrets yourself. Changing AUTH_SECRET leaves
# sessions and links working — nothing is derived from it — except that group
# settings can no longer display an invite link minted under the old secret,
# and offers a fresh one instead; changing POSTGRES_PASSWORD on an existing
# database locks you out of it, because the password is only applied when the
# cluster is created.
#
#   Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
#
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD may contain any characters: the container percent-encodes
# it before building the connection URL.
# AUTH_SECRET=
# POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Built from POSTGRES_PASSWORD automatically. Set it to point the app at a
# database outside this Compose project; then POSTGRES_PASSWORD is unused.
# DATABASE_URL=postgres://balancia:password@localhost:5432/balancia
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=10

# ── Push notifications ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Optional. Without these, notifications still appear inside the app; what you
# lose is push — reaching a device when Balancia is closed.
#
# Generate a pair with:  pnpm push:keys
#
# Note that push messages are relayed by the browser vendor's push service
# (Google, Mozilla, Apple). Payloads are encrypted end to end so the relay
# cannot read them, but it does see that a message reached a device. Leave
# these unset to keep the instance from talking to anyone.
#
# Delivery runs in the background worker: on a single-container install, set
# RUN_WORKER_IN_WEB=true as well.
# PUSH_VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
# PUSH_VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# PUSH_VAPID_SUBJECT=mailto:admin@example.com

# ── Receipt storage ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# "local" keeps receipts in the named Docker volume (default). "s3" uses any
# S3-compatible service.
STORAGE_DRIVER=local
# STORAGE_LOCAL_PATH=/data/uploads

# Maximum receipt upload size, in bytes. Default 10 MiB.
# UPLOAD_MAX_BYTES=10485760

# Required when STORAGE_DRIVER=s3:
# S3_BUCKET=balancia-receipts
# S3_REGION=eu-west-1
# S3_ENDPOINT=https://s3.example.com     # for MinIO, Garage, R2 …
# S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID=
# S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=
# S3_FORCE_PATH_STYLE=true               # usually true for self-hosted S3

# ── Email (optional) ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Without SMTP, Balancia works fully but does not offer email verification or
# password recovery.
# SMTP_HOST=smtp.example.com
# SMTP_PORT=587
# SMTP_USER=
# SMTP_PASSWORD=
# SMTP_SECURE=false
# SMTP_FROM=balancia@example.com

# ── Sign in with Apple (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default. Passwords and passkeys are unaffected; this only adds a
# second way in, and it is the one that involves a third party — Apple sees
# that someone signed in to this instance, and when.
#
# It also has prerequisites Balancia cannot supply: a paid Apple Developer
# Program membership, a Services ID whose return URL is
# https://your-domain/api/auth/apple/callback, and a .p8 sign-in key. Apple
# will not redirect to http:// or to localhost, so APP_URL must be the public
# HTTPS address. Set all four or none. Walkthrough in docs/self-hosting.md.
#
# APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY is the .p8 file's contents on one line, with the newlines
# written as \n:
#
#   APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY="$(awk '{printf "%s\\n", $0}' AuthKey_ABC1234567.p8)"
#
# APPLE_CLIENT_ID=com.example.balancia.web
# APPLE_TEAM_ID=A1B2C3D4E5
# APPLE_KEY_ID=ABC1234567
# APPLE_PRIVATE_KEY=

# ── Exchange rates (optional) ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default: nothing leaves the instance and rates are typed by hand.
# Set to "frankfurter" to have converted-currency groups suggest the day's
# rate (no API key, cached in your database). Frankfurter's v2 API blends the
# figures published by some eighty central banks, covering 165 currencies.
# The URL must be a v2 root: v1 is the ECB alone, which prices only thirty,
# and the app refuses to start pointed at it.
# EXCHANGE_RATE_PROVIDER=frankfurter
# EXCHANGE_RATE_API_URL=https://api.frankfurter.dev/v2

# ── Expense categorization ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Categories are suggested from rules that ship with Balancia, with no
# configuration and no outbound requests. This adds a semantic fallback for
# descriptions the rules do not cover; inference runs in the browser against
# model files this instance serves, so no transaction text leaves the device.
# Requires `pnpm semantic:install --yes` (~150 MB) and relaxes the CSP with
# 'wasm-unsafe-eval'. See docs/categorization.md.
# SEMANTIC_CATEGORIZATION=true

# ── Receipt scanning ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Reads a photographed receipt into an expense: merchant, date, line items and
# total, which you then correct and assign to people. Attaching the receipt to
# the expense afterwards is separate, and stores the image on this server as it
# always did.
# This switch only decides whether receipts are read at all. Which reader does
# it — the browser, or a provider this server calls — is the next block's
# business, and it is what decides whether an image is ever uploaded.
# See docs/receipt-scanning.md.
# RECEIPT_SCANNING=true

# ── Receipt reader ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# How a photographed receipt is read. Both readers can be on at once; the
# person scanning then picks per scan, and the interface says where the photo
# is going. See docs/receipt-scanning.md.
#
# On the device: PP-OCRv6 tiny in the browser. The image is never uploaded to
# be read. Needs `pnpm ocr:install --yes` (~32 MB) and relaxes the CSP with
# 'wasm-unsafe-eval'. On by default; set false to read only via a provider,
# which then needs neither the download nor the CSP change.
# RECEIPT_OCR_LOCAL=true
#
# Through a provider: this server sends the image to the reader you name.
# Not a replacement for the on-device reader, which handles an ordinary
# receipt well and for free. This is for what a 6 MB model cannot do:
# handwriting, unusual layouts, scripts outside its dictionary — and for
# getting structure back rather than text a parser has to interpret.
# One of: anthropic, gemini, mistral, openai.
#
# "openai" is the protocol, not the vendor — point RECEIPT_OCR_BASE_URL at
# your own Ollama or vLLM and the image stays on your hardware. On current
# open-weight document models that is also the most accurate and the cheapest
# option; docs/receipt-scanning.md compares them with numbers.
# RECEIPT_OCR_PROVIDER=none
# RECEIPT_OCR_API_KEY=
# RECEIPT_OCR_BASE_URL=
# A model is required for openai and gemini; anthropic and mistral default.
# For mistral the default tracks the newest generation at $4/1000 pages; the
# one before it is $2/1000 and reads a receipt about as well.
# RECEIPT_OCR_MODEL=

# ── Telemetry ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Balancia sends nothing from a self-hosted installation by default.
#
# TELEMETRY_MODE is the deployment's ceiling, not a switch. It can only ever
# subtract: there is no value of it that starts sending data, because the
# decision to share belongs to an administrator, in the UI, where it can be
# read and reversed.
#
#   opt-in (default)  An administrator may switch anonymous statistics on in
#                     Settings → Administration → Telemetry. Until one does,
#                     nothing is recorded and nothing is sent.
#   local             Counters are recorded on this server and never
#                     transmitted. The administration page can still preview
#                     the report that would be sent.
#   off               No telemetry at all. Stored opt-ins are ignored and the
#                     switches are disabled with an explanation.
#
# What an opted-in instance sends is one aggregated report a week: the Balancia
# version, the deployment kind, the CPU architecture, which optional features
# are on, and ranges — never counts — of how much happened. No expense amounts,
# no names, no group names, no receipts, no identifiers, no hostname, no URL,
# and no installation identifier of any kind. Full list: docs/telemetry.md.
# TELEMETRY_MODE=off

# Crash reports are a separate opt-in, also off until an administrator turns
# them on. Set this to false to take that decision away from the UI entirely.
# TELEMETRY_CRASH_REPORTS=false

# Where both switches start, until an administrator moves one. The only
# telemetry setting here that is a state rather than a ceiling — and the
# weakest kind: the first time somebody moves a switch in the application,
# their answer is stored and this stops applying to that switch for good,
# including when they move it back off.
#
# It is false by default because a fresh install should send nothing until
# somebody has been asked. ./scripts/bootstrap.sh does ask, which is what makes
# a "yes" here a decision rather than a surprise. Setting it true starts an
# instance with usage reporting on, and crash reporting too unless the line
# above forbids them; with TELEMETRY_MODE=off it still sends nothing.
# TELEMETRY_DEFAULT=true

# There is no setting for where reports go, on purpose. The address is a
# constant in src/lib/telemetry/endpoint.ts, so this file decides whether
# anything is sent and never to whom — an endpoint that could be set here, or
# in the admin UI, would be a request-forgery primitive pointed at your own
# network, and it would make every claim in docs/telemetry.md conditional on
# nobody having changed it. A fork edits that one line.

# How this instance is deployed, for the one coarse field that reports it.
# Compose sets it; elsewhere it is detected. docker-compose | docker |
# standalone | development
# TELEMETRY_DEPLOYMENT=docker

# Run the *collector*: accept reports from other installations at
# /api/telemetry/v1/report and /v1/crash. Off everywhere but the deployment
# that is telemetry.balancia.app; the routes answer 404 while it is off.
# TELEMETRY_RECEIVER=true

# ── Metrics (optional) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Prometheus metrics at /api/metrics: request durations, job durations and
# failures, database latency and pool usage, memory and CPU. Exact, local, and
# never transmitted by Balancia — the only way they leave this server is your
# own scraper.
#
# The app port is published, so set METRICS_TOKEN unless that port is on a
# private network. With metrics on and no token, anyone who can reach the app
# can read them.
# METRICS_ENABLED=true
# METRICS_TOKEN=

# ── Instance policy ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Set to false to close sign-ups on a private instance.
ALLOW_REGISTRATION=true

# fatal | error | warn | info | debug | trace
LOG_LEVEL=info

# Host port the app is published on.
# APP_PORT=3000
